# grund settings for `docker compose up`. Copy to .env and uncomment what you
# change; compose reads .env on its own. Nothing here is required: the
# defaults give a working instance at http://localhost:8080, whose first
# account comes from `docker compose exec grund /grund setup-link`, with no
# mail server.
#
# `grund serve --help` is the full reference; every flag has the variable
# named here. Secrets are generated on first start (the grund-data volume), so
# none needs to be typed in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- compose

# A published image instead of building this checkout, e.g.
# git.kjuulh.io/grund/grund:main-<commit>. There is no moving `latest` tag.
# Set both lines together: `build` (the default) rebuilds this checkout on
# every `up`, `missing` pulls the image named here.
# The commit is the full 40 characters; no image is tagged with a short one.
# GRUND_IMAGE=git.kjuulh.io/grund/grund:main-<commit>
# GRUND_PULL_POLICY=missing

# Where plain http is published on this machine. 127.0.0.1 keeps it local;
# for other machines and browsers, use GRUND_DOMAIN below (README,
# "Self-hosting").
# GRUND_BIND=127.0.0.1
# GRUND_PORT=8080
# GRUND_MAILPIT_PORT=8025

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- https

# The public name of this instance. Its DNS A/AAAA record must point at this
# machine, and port 443 must reach it from the internet: grund then orders a
# certificate by ACME (TLS-ALPN-01, answered on 443 itself; no port 80), keeps
# it in PostgreSQL and renews it when the CA's renewal information says, and
# serves https://<GRUND_DOMAIN> on GRUND_TLS_PORT. GRUND_PUBLIC_URL follows.
# GRUND_DOMAIN=grund.example.com
# GRUND_TLS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# GRUND_TLS_PORT=443
#
# The CA. Let's Encrypt production is the default; using it agrees to its
# subscriber agreement. Its limits apply per registered domain: 50 new
# certificates per 7 days, 5 per exact set of names per 7 days, 5 failed
# validations per name per hour (grund waits 5 minutes after the first
# failure, doubling to 12 hours). Renewals that follow the CA's renewal
# information are exempt. Set it empty to turn ACME off when you supply your
# own certificate (GRUND_TLS_CERT_FILE and GRUND_TLS_KEY_FILE, mounted into
# the container by a compose.override.yaml; self-hosting.md shows one).
# GRUND_ACME_DIRECTORY=https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
# GRUND_ACME_CONTACT=ops@example.com
# GRUND_ACME_PROFILE=classic

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the instance

# The address people use to reach grund, exactly as they type it: links in
# mail point here and forms from anywhere else are refused. Plain http is
# accepted only for localhost (or with GRUND_DEV_MODE=true). With
# GRUND_DOMAIN it is https://<GRUND_DOMAIN>; set it only to add a port, or
# when a proxy of your own serves grund on its own name:
# GRUND_PUBLIC_URL=https://grund.example.com
# GRUND_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Single (default): setup-link creates the first owner of one organisation;
# later accounts join by invitation. Multi: setup-link creates the first
# owner of the reserved admin organisation and the owner's own organisation;
# later sign-ups get their own organisation while signup is enabled. Existing
# instances may pin an operator organisation by UUID with
# GRUND_OPERATOR_ORGANISATION until their owner renames it to admin.
# GRUND_ORGANISATIONS=single

# Whether sign-up without an invitation is open at all, with multi.
# GRUND_SIGNUP_ENABLED=true
#
# Public app address policy: shared (default) uses GRUND_APP_DOMAIN to make
# <app>-<org>.<domain>. custom-only never issues a shared address; a private
# app must first bind a verified domain before a public release is accepted.
# This avoids serving customer apps under a domain not on the Public Suffix
# List. It does not configure DNS, an edge or certificate issuance for you.
# GRUND_APP_DOMAINS=custom-only

# Development only: allows plain http on a network address and a throwaway
# secret key. Never on an instance people depend on.
# GRUND_DEV_MODE=false

# Log format (compact or json) and filter.
# GRUND_LOG_FORMAT=json
# RUST_LOG=grund=info,grund_server=info,grund_store=info,notmad=info,warn

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- storage

# App and volume deletion fences use immediately. An owner may restore the
# original app and its managed volumes before this deadline; afterward
# physical cleanup runs (which may wait for an offline machine). Hours 1–720.
# GRUND_VOLUME_DELETE_WINDOW=168

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- machines

# The Machines page's "Add a machine" gives a one-liner that installs grund
# and its agent on a Linux machine (root, systemd, x86_64) and connects it:
#   curl -fsSL <GRUND_PUBLIC_URL>/install | sudo sh -s -- --url <GRUND_PUBLIC_URL> --code grund_join_... --from-instance
# grund serves that script and its own binary itself (true, compose's
# default), so it works for a published image and for a build of this
# checkout alike. A machine on another host needs GRUND_PUBLIC_URL to be
# https: `grund join` sends plain http only to localhost.
# GRUND_SERVE_INSTALLER=true
#
# Or another installer, with GRUND_SERVE_INSTALLER=false (not both). grund's
# own takes the build the instance names from grund's package registry, so it
# works only with a published GRUND_IMAGE, and the commit in both must be the
# full 40 characters (a short one is not found). Pin the script to that same
# commit:
# GRUND_SERVE_INSTALLER=false
# The forge serves a file at a commit through its API; it answers 403 to
# raw/commit/<commit>/... URLs.
# GRUND_AGENT_INSTALL_URL=https://git.kjuulh.io/api/v1/repos/grund/grund/raw/crates/grund-agent/install.sh?ref=<commit>

# Following is available from alpha.8. Legacy agents need one explicit
# feature-capable installer rerun; changing this setting cannot bootstrap them.
#
# Stop new machine-update offers and authorizations in this server process.
# An already authorized attempt still resolves safely. This extra guard does
# not change durable rollout state; use /grund agent-updates pause/resume with
# this database for the instance-wide pause shared by all server replicas.
# GRUND_AGENT_UPDATES_PAUSED=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mail

# Where mail goes. Unset (the default), nothing is sent: mail waits in the
# outbox, and readiness reports `mail` unhealthy once any is waiting. The
# first account needs none; password reset and invitations do, and a
# waiting mail is delivered once this is set, while its link still works.
# Examples:
#   smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587?tls=required   (STARTTLS)
#   smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465               (TLS)
# GRUND_SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587?tls=required
# GRUND_MAIL_FROM=grund <grund@example.com>
#
# Or catch every mail in Mailpit, which delivers nowhere (to try grund, not
# for real users), at http://localhost:8025 (GRUND_MAILPIT_PORT):
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=mail
# GRUND_SMTP_URL=smtp://mailpit:1025

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- limits

# How many reverse proxies in front of grund append X-Forwarded-For. Exactly
# the number you run; 0 uses the connecting address.
# GRUND_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Failed sign-ins per account name per 15 minutes before a 15-minute lock.
# GRUND_LOGIN_FAILURES_PER_ACCOUNT=10
# Sign-in attempts per client address per 15 minutes (0 = no per-address limit).
# GRUND_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS_PER_ADDRESS=100
# Reset and verification mails per address per hour.
# GRUND_MAIL_REQUESTS_PER_EMAIL=3
# Sign-up, reset and resend requests per client address per hour (0 = off).
# GRUND_MAIL_REQUESTS_PER_ADDRESS=20

# Session lifetime, in hours: idle timeout and absolute maximum.
# GRUND_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT=168
# GRUND_SESSION_MAX_AGE=720

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- license

# Commercial features (social sign-in) need a grund license key. Without one,
# grund runs every free feature and ignores the social settings below.
# GRUND_LICENSE_KEY=

# Social sign-in. Create the OAuth app at the provider with the callback
# <GRUND_PUBLIC_URL>/auth/<provider>/callback (github, google or oidc).
# GRUND_SOCIAL_LOGIN=false
# GRUND_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GRUND_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GRUND_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GRUND_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GRUND_OIDC_ISSUER=https://sso.example.com
# GRUND_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# GRUND_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GRUND_OIDC_NAME=Single sign-on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- tuning

# GRUND_DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=10
# GRUND_DATABASE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=5
# GRUND_DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=10
# GRUND_WORK_POLL_INTERVAL=5
# GRUND_HEALTH_INTERVAL=5
# GRUND_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=15
# GRUND_SHUTDOWN_GRACE=10
# GRUND_NATS_SUBJECT_PREFIX=grund

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- reporting

# Grund sends no account events unless both settings are present. On email
# confirmation it queues one event for the account in the same transaction,
# then POSTs it to this complete operator-selected HTTP endpoint after commit.
# The JSON includes account_id, username, email, method, instance,
# registered_at and verified_at. Delivery is at least once; the receiver
# deduplicates by (instance, account_id). Unset for self-hosted instances.
# GRUND_ACCOUNT_EVENT_URL=
# GRUND_ACCOUNT_EVENT_TOKEN=

# Optional external billing service (proto/grund/billing/v1). Without both
# settings, every organisation is free and nothing is sent to billing.
# GRUND_BILLING_URL=http://billing:8080
# GRUND_BILLING_TOKEN=
# GRUND_BILLING_MODE=sandbox
# GRUND_BILLING_REDIRECT_ORIGINS=
#
# New checkout and plan changes are disabled by default in either environment.
# Explicit live mode is not permission to sell. Turning new live sales off
# preserves payment repair, cancellation and existing provider obligations.
# GRUND_BILLING_SANDBOX_CHECKOUT=false
# GRUND_BILLING_LIVE_CHECKOUT=false
